<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gottlob Curt Heinrich von Tottleben</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1715–1773</span> · Also known as Count Tottleben</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gottlob Curt Heinrich von Tottleben was a Saxon-born general in Russian service. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1760 but was later convicted of secret dealings with the Prussians. Restored to service, he commanded the Russian expeditionary force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1769–1771 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774">Russo-Turkish War</a>, campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-i-of-imereti">Solomon I of Imereti</a> before quarrelling with both and being recalled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gottlob-curt-heinrich-von-tottleben.jpg" alt="Gottlob Curt Heinrich von Tottleben" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Гравюра Ивана Ивановича Матюшина · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gotlob-Kurt-Henrikh_Tottleben.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
